Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <regex>
#include <string>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex regex(R"(<p>.*?)</p>)");
    std::regex_replace(html, html, regex, "<p>$1<p>");
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a paragraph.\nThis is another paragraph.\nThis is a header.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to replace the `<p>` tags in the markdown text with `<p>` tags in the HTML text. The regular expression `R"(<p>.*?)</p>)"` matches the `<p>` tags and captures the text between them. The `std::regex_replace` function is used to replace the captured text with `<p>$1<p>`, where `$1` refers to the captured text.

The `main` function demonstrates how to use the function and prints the result.

